WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.330
You can feel the energy
&gt;&gt; Innovations.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.250
&gt;&gt; Silicon Valley.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.230
&gt;&gt; Design thinking.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.870
&gt;&gt; Globally recognized experts.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.580
&gt;&gt; It's truly diverse group.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.710
&gt;&gt; A new way of thinking that I
can take back to our corporation.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:20.160
&gt;&gt; Part of the issue that managers
face is that the world changes.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.945
It's changing so rapidly that the folks
who are actually doing the managing don't

00:00:23.945 --> 00:00:27.008
have time to sort of sit back and
think how might I do this better.

00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:30.130
&gt;&gt; We've become very biased
in our decision-making,

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.550
in our assessments,
based on the environment we live in.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:37.412
And so it's incredibly important to step
back, to step out of that comfort area,

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:40.794
hear different perspectives,
hear different ideas and

00:00:40.794 --> 00:00:44.325
do a check on what do I really
know versus what I think I know.

00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:46.951
[MUSIC]

00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:51.586
&gt;&gt; There is an atmosphere here that
fosters innovation, creativity and

00:00:51.586 --> 00:00:56.138
allows you to get focused and
really concentrated in the learning and

00:00:56.138 --> 00:00:58.990
in sharing experiences with the friends.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.120
Every day, I see people thinking
about new ways to do things.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.490
&gt;&gt; How do we serve customers?

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:04.530
How do we make money?

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.568
How do we make sure we
create a great culture?

00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:10.738
And so the ability in a very intense
period of time where you really are able

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:15.125
to share best practices and build a great
network of people around the world.

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:19.428
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:01:19.428 --> 00:01:23.202
&gt;&gt; My research largely
focuses on collective action,

00:01:23.202 --> 00:01:29.130
how to get groups of people together to
innovate, to undertake large changes.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.430
&gt;&gt; How do I scale my top management team?

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.775
We'll actually look at how do
you change a successful company?

00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:37.583
Been hard to do.

00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:40.780
[MUSIC]

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.799
&gt;&gt; The warmth that is provided from the
Stanford professors is really endearing.

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:49.481
[MUSIC]

00:01:49.481 --> 00:01:53.643
&gt;&gt; In my negotiation class, I talk first
about the negotiation process itself

00:01:53.643 --> 00:01:56.439
beyond an adversarial
win-lose kind of dynamic,

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:00.743
to try to get them to think about not
only value creation, but value claiming.

00:02:00.743 --> 00:02:04.711
&gt;&gt; I have teams in five different
countries and seeing the different people

00:02:04.711 --> 00:02:08.494
and their attitude towards negotiations
was very important for me.

00:02:08.494 --> 00:02:11.395
[MUSIC]

00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:16.154
&gt;&gt; Every time you think you cracked
the case, or you are on top of the issue,

00:02:16.154 --> 00:02:21.270
you see another brilliant or
different or surprising point of view.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:25.080
&gt;&gt; I once had an executive say to me, this
is a really interesting idea you have but

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.720
I could never take the time to do this.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.670
And my response was because you're so
time stressed,

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.000
you absolutely need to be able to do this.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.260
&gt;&gt; You have to stop and ask yourself, are
you really always at the top of your game?

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.090
Having that opportunity to step aside and
think about things differently, we get so

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.826
entrenched in our everyday work,
it's hard.

00:02:44.826 --> 00:02:48.735
We're just trying to keep the game
going and this allows you the chance to

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:52.213
step back and say, is there
a different way of playing the game?

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:53.480
And there is.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:03:03.480
[MUSIC]

